        <section class="project-content-section project-two-column">

            <div>

                <p class="section-label">01 · Project Overview</p>

                <h2>Redesigning a Product, Not Just a Part</h2>

            </div>


            <div class="project-body-text">

                <p>
                    The objective of MANU 330 was to take an existing RC vehicle
                    and improve it through a series of interconnected mechanical
                    and manufacturing redesigns.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Our final vehicle combined four major custom subsystems:
                    cast aluminum wheels, a self-righting roll cage,
                    a thermoformed ABS aeroshell, and an adjustable
                    chassis extension.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Each subsystem progressed through concept generation,
                    material and process selection, prototyping, simulation,
                    physical testing, redesign, manufacturing planning,
                    cost estimation, and final vehicle integration.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Rather than treating the project as four isolated parts,
                    the final stage focused on assembling them into a single
                    manufacturable product and developing a production strategy
                    for a hypothetical 20,000-unit manufacturing run.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Four Systems -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-highlight-section">

            <p class="section-label">02 · System Redesign</p>

            <h2>Four Subsystems, Four Manufacturing Problems</h2>


            <div class="project-feature-grid">

                <article>

                    <h3>Cast Aluminum Wheels</h3>

                    <p>
                        Redesigned the stock wheels for sand casting,
                        including geometry, draft, gating, risers,
                        pattern development, structural validation,
                        and tire-retention features.
                    </p>

                </article>


                <article>

                    <h3>Self-Righting Roll Cage</h3>

                    <p>
                        Developed a protective roll structure that used
                        vehicle geometry and center-of-mass effects to
                        return the car upright after inversion.
                    </p>

                </article>


                <article>

                    <h3>Thermoformed Aeroshell</h3>

                    <p>
                        Designed a Kei-truck-inspired ABS body shell
                        and forming tool while studying draft,
                        fillets, material thinning, webbing,
                        trimming, and part release.
                    </p>

                </article>


                <article>

                    <h3>Adjustable Chassis Extension</h3>

                    <p>
                        Designed a lightweight structural extension
                        to alter vehicle length and realign interfaces,
                        with multiple mounting positions and validated
                        bolted connections.
                    </p>

                </article>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Wheels -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-two-column">

            <div>

                <p class="section-label">03 · Aluminum Wheel Development</p>

                <h2>Designing for Sand Casting</h2>

                <div class="image-placeholder">
                    Wheel CAD + Casting / Mold Photos
                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="project-body-text">

                <p>
                    The wheel redesign required balancing structural performance,
                    castability, tire retention, low mass, and repeatable
                    manufacturing.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Multiple wheel geometries were compared before selecting
                    a five-circle design with smoother transitions and more
                    uniform wall thicknesses to improve load distribution
                    and mold filling.
                </p>


                <h3>Simulation & Material Selection</h3>

                <p>
                    A356 aluminum was selected through a weighted material
                    decision process. FEA of a severe impact-and-rotation
                    loading case predicted a maximum stress of approximately
                    2.25 MPa, compared with an assumed material yield strength
                    of approximately 165 MPa.
                </p>


                <h3>Learning Through Failed Castings</h3>

                <p>
                    Early casting attempts exposed practical problems that
                    were not obvious in CAD. These included insufficient
                    feature depth, sand damage, runner placement, and
                    incomplete mold formation.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The design and casting process were revised between
                    attempts, ultimately producing three successful parts
                    in the final casting run.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Roll Cage -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-highlight-section">

            <div class="project-two-column">

                <div>

                    <p class="section-label">04 · Self-Righting Roll Cage</p>

                    <h2>Turning a Failed Test Into a Functional Design</h2>

                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        Self-Righting Test / Roll Cage CAD
                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="project-body-text">

                    <p>
                        One of the project's clearest design iterations came
                        from the roll cage.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The first halo-style prototype successfully protected
                        the vehicle but failed the self-righting requirement,
                        leaving the car balanced on two wheels during the
                        worst-case inverted test.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The geometry was then widened to increase the effective
                        lever arm and alter how the vehicle's center of mass
                        moved during rotation.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The revised configuration consistently returned the
                        car upright from the tested fully inverted condition.
                    </p>


                    <h3>Structural Design</h3>

                    <p>
                        A conservative approximately 400 N impact case was
                        evaluated using FEA. The resulting peak stress was
                        approximately 43 MPa against an assumed ABS yield
                        strength near 48 MPa.
                    </p>


                    <h3>Production Concept</h3>

                    <p>
                        The final cage was designed as a modular injection-molded
                        ABS assembly consisting of two halo components and a base,
                        with an estimated production cycle of approximately
                        9 minutes 50 seconds per cage.
                    </p>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Aeroshell -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-two-column">

            <div>

                <p class="section-label">05 · Thermoformed Aeroshell</p>

                <h2>From Mold Geometry to Production Shell</h2>

                <div class="image-placeholder">
                    Aeroshell Mold + Thermoformed Final Shell
                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="project-body-text">

                <p>
                    The aeroshell was designed around vacuum thermoforming
                    of 0.030-inch ABS sheet, requiring the body geometry
                    to be developed alongside the manufacturing process.
                </p>


                <h3>Process Development</h3>

                <p>
                    Small-scale forming experiments were used to evaluate
                    draft angle, corner radii, mold height, female features,
                    venting, release behaviour, and webbing before committing
                    to the full vehicle mold.
                </p>


                <h3>ANSYS PolyFlow</h3>

                <p>
                    Thermoforming simulations were used to examine material
                    flow, local thinning, and webbing. Mold geometry was then
                    revised with larger fillets and smoother draw transitions
                    before the final design was manufactured.
                </p>


                <h3>Production Planning</h3>

                <p>
                    The scaled process used a multi-part forming layout and
                    identified trimming as the primary production bottleneck.
                    The estimated cycle time was approximately 6 minutes
                    per shell, with an estimated cost of about $5.6 per part.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Chassis -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-highlight-section">

            <div class="project-two-column">

                <div>

                    <p class="section-label">06 · Chassis Extension</p>

                    <h2>Design, Material Selection & Physical Validation</h2>

                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        Chassis Extension CAD + Installed Component
                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="project-body-text">

                    <p>
                        The chassis extension was developed to modify the vehicle's
                        overall length while maintaining alignment with the stock
                        chassis and allowing multiple mounting positions.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The final design used PA6-GF25 chopped glass-fiber-reinforced
                        nylon and a C-channel geometry manufactured through
                        additive manufacturing.
                    </p>


                    <h3>Structural Validation</h3>

                    <p>
                        FEA predicted a maximum stress of approximately 21 MPa
                        and a minimum factor of safety greater than 7.
                    </p>


                    <h3>Fastener Testing</h3>

                    <p>
                        Physical tests were used to determine the required
                        bolted connection. Two bolts produced excessive
                        movement, three improved the joint, and four created
                        a rigid connection during vehicle testing.
                    </p>


                    <h3>Production Scale-Up</h3>

                    <p>
                        The finalized process required approximately
                        35.8 minutes of print time per part. A conceptual
                        40-printer farm operating at 90% utilization was
                        estimated to produce approximately 67 parts per hour,
                        at a calculated cost of approximately $4.42 per part.
                    </p>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


