            <h2>Developing the Product Through Multiple Processes</h2>


            <div class="project-feature-grid">

                <article>
                    <h3>Sand Casting</h3>
                    <p>
                        Pattern development, gating and riser design,
                        sand molding, aluminum casting, defect analysis,
                        and iterative process improvement.
                    </p>
                </article>


                <article>
                    <h3>Thermoforming</h3>
                    <p>
                        Mold design, draft and fillet development,
                        vacuum forming, venting, trimming,
                        webbing analysis, and production simulation.
                    </p>
                </article>


                <article>
                    <h3>Injection Molding Design</h3>
                    <p>
                        Designed the final roll cage around scalable
                        ABS injection molding, including modularity,
                        tooling assumptions, cycle time, and assembly.
                    </p>
                </article>


                <article>
                    <h3>Additive Manufacturing</h3>
                    <p>
                        Used 3D printing for functional prototypes,
                        production tooling, casting patterns,
                        and the final chassis-extension process.
                    </p>
                </article>


                <article>
                    <h3>Composite Processing</h3>
                    <p>
                        Manufactured and tensile-tested glass-fiber
                        composite coupons through vacuum-assisted
                        wet layup to compare a potential alternative
                        chassis manufacturing route.
                    </p>
                </article>


                <article>
                    <h3>DFMA & Costing</h3>
                    <p>
                        Considered tooling, labor, cycle time,
                        equipment utilization, assembly sequence,
                        manufacturing risk, and per-part cost
                        throughout the design process.
                    </p>
                </article>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Integration -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-two-column">

            <div>

                <p class="section-label">09 · Final Integration</p>

                <h2>Bringing the Subsystems Together</h2>

                <div class="image-placeholder">
                    Exploded Final Vehicle Assembly
                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="project-body-text">

                <p>
                    The final project stage moved beyond individual component
                    development and focused on the complete manufacturing
                    system.
                </p>

                <p>
                    An exploded CAD assembly was used to verify subsystem
                    fitment, fastener locations, clearances, and assembly order
                    before the complete vehicle was built.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A manufacturing workflow was then developed in which wheel
                    casting and machining, roll-cage molding, aeroshell
                    thermoforming, and chassis-extension printing occurred
                    in parallel before converging at final assembly.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This made the project particularly valuable because
                    engineering decisions were evaluated not only by whether
                    a prototype worked, but also by whether the design could
                    realistically be produced, assembled, and scaled.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- Lessons -->
        <section class="project-content-section project-highlight-section">

            <p class="section-label">10 · Engineering Lessons</p>

            <h2>What Made This Project Valuable</h2>


            <div class="project-three-column">

                <article>

                    <h3>Prototype Early</h3>

                    <p>
                        Several issues only appeared once parts were physically
                        manufactured. Casting defects, roll behaviour,
                        thermoforming release, and joint stiffness all required
                        real testing.
                    </p>

                </article>


                <article>

                    <h3>Failure Creates Better Designs</h3>

                    <p>
                        Failed castings and an unsuccessful first roll-cage
                        configuration became useful engineering inputs,
                        driving changes that produced successful later
                        iterations.
                    </p>

                </article>


                <article>

                    <h3>Manufacturing Shapes Design</h3>

                    <p>
                        Material choice, tooling, geometry, cycle time,
                        assembly, cost, and production volume cannot be
                        separated from the mechanical design itself.
                    </p>

                </article>

            </div>

        </section>


